{"content":{"id":1263,"headline":"Novel Treatment for Nasal Tumors","tagline":"Help us treat nasal tumors in dogs using a new kind of therapy!","description":"Nasal tumors can cause severe nasal bleeding or discharge and difficulty breathing. These tumors often aggressively spread throughout the tissues of the nasal cavity and into the surrounding organs, including the eye and brain. Radiation therapy is the treatment of choice for nasal tumors in dogs; however, radiation therapy isn't often good for long-term control of the tumor. An another treatment option is transarterial embolization, or TAE, which involves the delivery of a mechanical “clot” to the main arteries supplying blood to the tissues of the nasal cavity with the goal of eliminating the blood supply of the tumor and decreasing tumor size.

The purposes of this study are to 1) describe TAE in dogs with naturally-occurring nasal cancer, 2) evaluate the effect that nasal TAE has on the tumor, and 3) evaluate the effect that nasal TAE has on the values measured by positron emission tomography (PET) scanning.","requirements":"<p>If you agree for your dog to take part in this study, your dog will undergo:</p><p>* Transarterial embolization (TAE)</p><p>* A PET/CT scan. This will be repeated at 1 month post-TAE.</p>","who_can_participate":"We are looking for dogs diagnosed with nasal cancer.

To find out if your dog can take part in this study, your dog will need a CT scan. If the CT scan demonstrates that invasion of adjacent structures is present, your dog may not be able to be enrolled and you will need to cover the costs of that CT scan.","benefits_and_risks":"We hope that the data acquired in this study will allow us to advance the treatment of cancer in both dogs and people.","video_url":null,"age_range":null,"keywords":["Nasal Cancer","nasal","tumor","mass","cancer","dog","canine","nose","TAE","transarterial embolization"],"study_type":"Device","time_commitment":"Your dog will be in this study for approximately 4-5 weeks.","compensation":"The study will cover both of the PET/CT scans and associated anesthesia.","links":null,"logo_preview_url":"https://yuzu-studypages-prod-east.s3.amazonaws.com/org_logos/ucdavis-veterinary-medicine-logo.png","logo_preview_url_or_none":"https://yuzu-studypages-prod-east.s3.amazonaws.com/org_logos/ucdavis-veterinary-medicine-logo.png","banner_preview_url":"https://yuzu-studypages-prod-east.s3.amazonaws.com/study_banners/1518.jpg","start_date":"2019-02-25","end_date":null,"study_conditions":null,"contact_phone":"+15307521393","target_gender":null,"responsibilities":"<p>If you choose for your dog to take part in this study, you will need to:</p><p>* Bring dog in for all scheduled visits</p><p>* Cover costs of the TAE and any medical costs from any complications that may occur</p><p>* Complete owner questionnaires</p>","healthy_volunteer":"N","species":["dogs"],"is_active":true,"video_thumb_url":null,"contact_name":"Dr. William Culp","contact_email":"wculp@ucdavis.edu","study_refer_conditions":null,"recruitment_target":10,"locations":[{"lat":"38.5300685","lng":"-121.76376019999998","route":"Garrod Drive","address":"1 Garrod Drive, Davis, CA, USA","country":"United States","locality":"Davis","site_name":"UC Davis Veterinary Medical Teaching Hospital","postal_code":"95616","street_number":"1","address_line_2":"Small Anima